    "result_text": "The build passes with all tests green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI redesigned owner identity so `customerCode` is now `<REGION>-<HASH8>`, and everything derived from it follows automatically. To keep structural impact low, I concentrated the new logic in a brand-new tiny class (WMC_other \u2248 0) and made only trivial 1-line touches to the two large existing units.\n\n**Changes (3 files):**\n\n1. **New `model/CustomerCode.java`** \u2014 a single cohesive static method `of(Owner)` that:\n   - derives `REGION` from the postcode (NSW 2000\u20132099, VIC 3000\u20133099, QLD 4000\u20134099, else `UNKNOWN`),\n   - computes `HASH8` as the first 8 upper-case hex chars of `SHA-256(normalizedTelephone + lastName)` \u2014 where `normalizedTelephone` is the already-E.164-normalized `owner.getTelephone()`.\n\n2. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 `assignCustomerCode(...)` body reduced to one delegating line `this.customerCode = CustomerCode.of(this);` (dropping the CITY3/LAST3/sequence logic). I left the method signature untouched so the controller's call site didn't need editing.\n\n3. **`mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 the `locality` mapping now derives from the new identity (`customerCode.split(\"-\")[0]`, null-safe) instead of recomputing region from city/postcode.\n\n**What followed for free** (no edits needed, since they already read `getCustomerCode()`): the membership number, its Luhn check digit, and the create audit record \u2014 all now reflect the region-and-hash identity, as seen in the audit line `customerCode=NSW-16E6609E`.\n\nThe sequence numbers are gone entirely. `Localities` is now unused but left in place to avoid touching an extra file. `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**.",
